WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.910
Meet the most audacious granddads this side
of a Sunday roast.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:11.619
The so called “grandpa gang” pulled off
the largest burglary in English history in

00:00:11.619 --> 00:00:12.820
April 2015.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:18.790
The group of four men robbed a safety deposit
building in the world famous Hatton Garden

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:22.760
area of London – well known for its jewelry
and diamond stores.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:28.640
The gang hobbled away with £14 million worth
of diamonds, jewelry and cash.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.800
But let’s not all get too excited, these
weren’t your average grandpas, who got overly

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.890
frustrated at bingo and decided to plan a
grand larceny out of the blue.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.769
Every member of the gang was a seasoned criminal
who had a long rap list of armed robberies

00:00:42.769 --> 00:00:43.800
between them.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.870
Hey, with life expectancy’s rising along
with the retirement age, you can’t blame

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.590
them for not wanting to hang it up just yet.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:55.600
The ringleader and mastermind of the plot
is a man codenamed “Basil” – the group

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.879
originally settled on Mr. Pink, but he was
having none of that.

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:05.150
Other members of the group were nicknamed
“Mr. Ginger, Mr. Strong, Mr. Montana, The

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.460
Gent, The Tall Man and The Old Man”.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.060
This sounds like a movie just waiting to happen.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.770
It was a well orchestrated, ambitious and
daring plan, all thought up by a group of

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.950
cockney granddads in a pub in Islington, seriously
thought it’s like the cast of Snatch have

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.020
returned for a reunion 30 years later.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:28.320
The youngest of the group was 48 years-old,
whilst the rest where in their 50s, 60s and

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.229
70s, the eldest being ringleader Brian Reader
at 76.

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:38.100
Better yet, Brian Reader actually used his
senior citizen’s buss pass to get to the

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.100
scene of the crime.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.810
You’ve got to admire these gent’s tenacity.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:47.119
It’s believed eleven men where involved
in the planning of the burglary, but only

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:51.630
four of the men actually carried out the theft,
those four also happened to be the eldest

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.840
and most experienced of the group.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.340
So how did they do it?

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.600
Well it was no small feat, the audacious plan
was three years in the making.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.210
To start, the burglary was perfectly timed
to take place on the long Easter weekend,

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.899
a four-day holiday in the UK.

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:13.520
This gave them four full days to perform the
theft when they knew the safety deposit boxes

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.220
would not be manned.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:20.560
The main man, Basil, who’s identity is still
unknown, was an insider who the police believed

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.390
either worked at or had strong connections
with Hatton Garden Safe Deposit LTD, for months

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:30.090
or even years before the robbery took place.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:35.240
The four men approached the building disguised
as garbage collector’s pushing wheelie bins,

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.400
the bins of course having a double use, as
they were also used to transport the stolen

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.850
goods out of the building afterwards.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:46.370
The gang then used Basil’s insider knowledge
to enter the building via the elevator shaft,

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:51.740
they then climbed down to the basement and
drilled through the thick concrete wall of

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:55.810
the underground vault using an industrial
diamond-tipped power drill.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.220
And then they robbed the vault!

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:04.020
No… not quite, remember, this was a sophisticated,
elderly gentleman’s caper, this wasn’t

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.770
a smash and grab and they’re certainly not
in the hood.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:13.510
So they took their sweet time, after a full
day of drilling the men were too exhausted

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:20.010
to carry on and were foiled by a metal cabinet
inside the vault which they couldn’t shift.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:25.380
So they all went home for a cuppa and a crossword
presumably, then returned the next day, entered

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:33.450
the vault and stole 73 deposit boxes containing
£14 million worth of fine gemstones and luxury

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:34.450
goods.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:37.840
But the escapade wasn’t without its blunders.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.900
The daring old gents actually triggered the
alarm during the first day of the robbery.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.350
So surely that was game over?

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:49.710
No, security guards came but declared it was
a false alarm and left.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:54.010
“Bloody ‘ell guv’nor that was a close
one”, we can only imagine the gang remarked

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.640
before continuing along their merry way.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.890
This was a proper old-school caper.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:03.400
The gang used walkie-talkies to communicate
to avoid being tracked by the police.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.890
You won’t find this lot using burners and
fancy internet VPNs.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.670
So what became of this bunch of old-timers?

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:17.030
Well the gang may have gotten away with many
robberies in the past, but their old age must

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:21.709
have caught up to them as they left a few
clues with eventually led to the arrest of

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:25.380
all but one individual involved in the robbery.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:30.080
One of the police detective’s biggest clues
was that gang member Daniel Jones bought a

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.730
hydraulic pump in a specialist hardware shop
in Twickenham, 15 miles from the scene of

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:35.730
the crime.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:41.140
But the silly bugger only went and gave his
real name and address to the sales assistant.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.670
All-in-all the gang may have just been a bit
out of their depth and out of practice with

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:50.860
all the new fangled technology and techniques
the police now use to track down criminals.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.890
They were definitely trying to brush up on
it all as the police found a “Forensics

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:56.960
for Dummies” book in one of the men’s
apartments.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.710
You just couldn’t make this stuff up.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.660
So who was the one man that did get away?

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:08.290
It was of course the infamous, the incredible,
the main man at the heart of it all, “Basil”.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:14.400
Whilst all his cohorts are now serving time,
to this day, the police still have no idea

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:20.240
as to the identity of whereabouts of Basil,
what they do know is that wherever he is,

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.670
he has a cool £10 million worth of diamonds
on him.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:29.450
Rumour has it he used his new found wealth
to start a hotel in Torquay with his wife

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:33.940
Sybil.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:56.410
I guess we’ll never know.

